  "notes": "LA's raw retro-soul shouter, \"the lane's closest thing to a 60s Stax voice\". `confidence` is **low** — the research rates the entry medium-low, sourcing the lane position and the covers practice but marking the parameters as inference. The kit is \"live, played hard and dry — kick on 1 and 3, snare cracked on 2&4, tambourine doubling, triplet fills, and no 16th hat carpet\", so the kick anchors are literally 1 and 3 at a syncopation of 0.16, the velocity tiers are among the hardest in the section, the hat is an 8th at a fill density of 0.16, and `snareRoll.subdivisions` are triplets for the stated fills. \"Stop-time hits and a half-time double-length chorus are section devices\", which is `fillBeforeSection`. Harmony is \"major-mode blues-soul — 12-bar and `I–IV–V` forms with dominant 7ths and blues thirds in the melody\", giving the highest triad weight and nearly no ninths, plus a blues scale in the set and a raised `halfStepDissonance` for the third. Melody is stated as dynamics rather than range: \"mid-alto delivered at high volume with a hard rasp, short repeated phrases, heavy blues ornament and shouted interjections — the parameter is sustained loud dynamics rather than range\", which is the riff-loop structure over two-and-four-bar phrases with a narrow register. Bass is \"thick and short, root-and-blues-fill\". ⚠ Every drum, melody and bass number here is INFERRED. ⚠ Her chord families and her bass behaviour overlap Anthony Hamilton's because the research describes both as I–IV–V church-and-blues players; the separation between the two models is authored in tempo, kit hardness and hat behaviour rather than invented in the harmony.",
